  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>Iran attacked 10 commercial and naval vessels near the Strait of Hormuz on September 9 — the largest declared wave of tit-for-tat shipping attacks by either side since the conflict began. The United Kingdom Maritime Trade Operations confirmed multiple vessel strikes in the northern Gulf and the Gulf of Oman, with one ship listing near Port Rashid (UAE) and a second damaged southeast of al-Faw (Iraq). Iran attributed the operation to CENTCOM's September 8 destruction of five Iranian tankers (M/T Kaviz, Charminar, Horizon 1, Riesco, and Derya). → supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>

      <p>Houthi forces seized the Yemeni port city of Mocha on September 10 and advanced toward the Hanish Islands on the Red Sea coast, pushing Yemeni government forces south toward Dhubab — the town directly on the Bab el-Mandeb Strait entrance. Times of Israel, Euronews, and Foreign Policy all carried independent confirmation of the Mocha seizure; UNSC convened an emergency session on Yemen the same day. A Houthi spokesman confirmed the advance was part of a coordinated campaign with Iran. → moved <span class="cite">F9</span></p>

      <p>Brent crude was reported at $101.25–$104.41/bbl on September 10 across Trading Economics, Energy Intelligence, and Convex trade data, and at $102.59 on September 11. Two trading days are consistent with settlement above $100, though official ICE front-month settlement for September 10 had not appeared in a single authoritative Bloomberg or Reuters data print as of this writing. The 10-ship Hormuz attack, the IRGC restricted-zone coordinates publication, and the Houthi Mocha seizure each contributed to the sustained premium above $100 during the 48h window. → moved <span class="cite">F8</span></p>

      <p>The Israel Defense Forces completed demolition of the Hezbollah tunnel network at the Ali Taher ridge in southern Lebanon on September 10, detonating 1,100 tonnes of explosives across 5.4 kilometers of underground infrastructure and triggering a 4.1-magnitude earthquake confirmed by the USGS. IDF declared the southern Lebanon security zone operationally complete (The National; Times of Israel; France24). No Hezbollah military response was recorded within the following 24 hours. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>IRGC published coordinates of the restricted maritime zone on September 9 (announced September 7 by Security Council Secretary Rezaei), extending Iran's claimed interdiction perimeter from Chabahar through the Gulf of Oman — wider than the Strait of Hormuz proper. Under the zone's terms, any vessel entering faces IRGC sanctions and insurance blacklisting. A CBS correspondent reported overnight aircraft damage at Jordan's Muwaffaq Salti Air Base; CENTCOM had not confirmed attribution or damage extent as of September 11, leaving the F6 resolution criterion unmet as of this update. → supports <span class="cite">F6</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:68%;">Escalation 68%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:25%;">Protracted 25%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:7%;">De-esc 7%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> Driven by F6 (72% Iran strike on US asset by Sep 13) and Houthi Bab el-Mandeb advance (F9 at 20%), the most probable path is continued tit-for-tat kinetic exchange broadening to a second chokepoint.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path:</strong> Kinetic exchange sustains at Hormuz intensity without expanding to Bab el-Mandeb; IRGC holds next retaliatory cycle past Sep 13; oil stays elevated but below systemic disruption threshold.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> No diplomatic channel is open (F3 at 5%) and no ceasefire offer has moved past a conditional precondition; de-escalation requires a unilateral US or Iranian tactical pause not yet visible in any OSINT source.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · ~200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media (IRNA and Tasnim) framed the September 9 ten-vessel strike as a "measured and proportionate" response to CENTCOM's tanker destruction, attributing the full escalatory sequence to the United States. Tasnim ran the IRGC Navy Commander's statement under the headline "Iran's defensive response was within the law of the sea." Kayhan published an op-ed describing the attacks as "the minimum the IRGC owes to the nation's dignity" — language consistent with domestic legitimation of further operations rather than closure. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>State media's coverage of the restricted maritime zone was notably bureaucratic in tone: IRNA carried it as a routine maritime safety notification. Press TV broadcast a map of the zone coordinates alongside an infographic comparing it to US-declared exclusion zones in historical naval conflicts. The framing was calibrated toward international legal legitimacy — not toward deterrent threat language — which the IRGC's operational posture has not matched. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>

      <p>Pezeshkian's September 1 statement — "if the US returns to its MOU commitments, we will immediately reciprocate" — was replayed prominently in IRNA on September 10 alongside coverage of Trump's "Iran can't hold out after the midterms" remark. State media juxtaposed the two leaders without naming either by title: "one offering a path, one offering threats." The editorial choice preserved the conditional ceasefire offer in domestic circulation while the IRGC escalated at sea — a dual-track information posture the outlet has maintained since the June MOU collapse. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

